[{:db/id "q-162236/selection/55e76c41eb7a82dad37318f56f643e70a1357cee2f7583f7589cde4a45a496ed" :answer/id #uuid "5c0041a7-89cc-5de9-a27d-d628360922b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162236/selection/2ca8e3d5f831e4022cb4f333be18f86e4fb542d67611f6a8cfbb62901d0def92" :answer/id #uuid "126ee6ef-6165-57b6-97c8-664b9874c753" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162236/selection/ef13fe659d3134041eb8798fd261db362e2f5045f03933863460ea6cae22263b" :answer/id #uuid "04998379-a1f1-504a-9863-3cab2b7a3d10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162236/selection/b3489af90287baad7954880e1d45ef52f303de5c794d5d12e6bdd1334d13b732" :answer/id #uuid "d601aed8-7270-5e56-814b-f8674a8dc5b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162236/selection/657b33f57e7ca214fcef582c4921dd1f22421ba386c938e59426676f59dc7e2c" :answer/id #uuid "756884bf-a7d3-53d9-8b5e-67ac64c13127" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162236/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "04f0cf81-6deb-5a07-b103-b225b644ed8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162236/selection/55e76c41eb7a82dad37318f56f643e70a1357cee2f7583f7589cde4a45a496ed" :answer-field/choices ["q-162236/selection/55e76c41eb7a82dad37318f56f643e70a1357cee2f7583f7589cde4a45a496ed" "q-162236/selection/2ca8e3d5f831e4022cb4f333be18f86e4fb542d67611f6a8cfbb62901d0def92" "q-162236/selection/ef13fe659d3134041eb8798fd261db362e2f5045f03933863460ea6cae22263b" "q-162236/selection/b3489af90287baad7954880e1d45ef52f303de5c794d5d12e6bdd1334d13b732" "q-162236/selection/657b33f57e7ca214fcef582c4921dd1f22421ba386c938e59426676f59dc7e2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162236" :question/id #uuid "9383b3a9-2a6a-5e82-a4da-b4d00cab688d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162236" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le xy}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/6b3720af66e81aef6792d4b2a34b2bdf2b93bd59ed6e28e4b38a1e06964bb2cc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/9dc99cc0df9f089b9f62402c3c5ea9c4408be077debbf6dfadeb884ee4d8b2d0.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[z]_{0}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[{x}^{2}]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{4})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162236/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162236"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162250/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "b0a2e4c5-6464-5bc3-9cba-4413e7f21c84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162250/selection/bce5ea7f1aadd119ad7d8e92aa430b5d866c0162c1e7e77b59793647a4d2d519" :answer/id #uuid "dd71d117-be4d-5736-980a-8d5eeaedea69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162250/selection/b268aecc02e7c227c1c2e99ee33c454057c45375067cedb424901921e6c83790" :answer/id #uuid "71e427b7-ce60-5c33-80b8-33eb896da100" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162250/selection/e1a79fe21dbd63050cc5b8abf7bbfe646e6735d60cb33653e6f487991ef831d5" :answer/id #uuid "8fbebbe5-164c-5507-9979-faef0f7d3ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162250/selection/ace37c2040207cebb14b55c05840a4c383db24d63f2349cf49f8db6a85f5faeb" :answer/id #uuid "2a359fd9-52a3-59a1-a43b-54f2913795e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162250/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6be2c5db-bc7b-5e26-9d57-b6b887c43d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162250/selection/ace37c2040207cebb14b55c05840a4c383db24d63f2349cf49f8db6a85f5faeb" :answer-field/choices ["q-162250/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-162250/selection/bce5ea7f1aadd119ad7d8e92aa430b5d866c0162c1e7e77b59793647a4d2d519" "q-162250/selection/b268aecc02e7c227c1c2e99ee33c454057c45375067cedb424901921e6c83790" "q-162250/selection/e1a79fe21dbd63050cc5b8abf7bbfe646e6735d60cb33653e6f487991ef831d5" "q-162250/selection/ace37c2040207cebb14b55c05840a4c383db24d63f2349cf49f8db6a85f5faeb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162250" :question/id #uuid "3f89ba26-5dec-5dd6-9021-9b421647a5f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162250" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/38eb7316b1ee5c947eee37a9cc10530ff08f793b7564cf0d88c6ce24e1239f9d.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/241abff7fae5d6ba3301d605ff4c4e62ff61e423515c7bc3a54b17b9aec525aa.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[z]_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-(1-e) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162250/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162250"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162322/selection/80a4a48d1946be0863b126e0f8469ef99d63c134cf6856fda75794f471366d7a" :answer/id #uuid "6c0776f3-d984-5b03-9583-5f53c92af266" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=2x,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162322/selection/03d3e4d8c146524608aaa7efb88cf1d56d0a080e7b29dde12c5578ac96be6b89" :answer/id #uuid "108be47a-4b65-5e38-9757-e88fedddeff6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162322/selection/578f21fddbaeae4abc71e3a999605b3ce3306481717f0641e3e0617e9159f3e0" :answer/id #uuid "d2cbe476-e1f7-55af-a343-2aec7c66279f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{x},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162322/selection/85f10286a74d6a491034164243900939a00966383e914dc0a962ab46aff5532b" :answer/id #uuid "1b962d52-e18a-597c-9a07-375c4fb8f919" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p={x}^{2},\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162322/selection/e239da2a207afa1f549cb74524b3b4fd998c233bc1c366f72d87a4bca1fa7e54" :answer/id #uuid "e4faaba5-05d2-5f8e-855c-4cdfcb933599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=2x,\\,\\,\\,q=e,\\,\\,\\,r={e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162322/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee971d1-e5fa-579d-859c-0e1f92aa1289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162322/selection/85f10286a74d6a491034164243900939a00966383e914dc0a962ab46aff5532b" :answer-field/choices ["q-162322/selection/80a4a48d1946be0863b126e0f8469ef99d63c134cf6856fda75794f471366d7a" "q-162322/selection/03d3e4d8c146524608aaa7efb88cf1d56d0a080e7b29dde12c5578ac96be6b89" "q-162322/selection/578f21fddbaeae4abc71e3a999605b3ce3306481717f0641e3e0617e9159f3e0" "q-162322/selection/85f10286a74d6a491034164243900939a00966383e914dc0a962ab46aff5532b" "q-162322/selection/e239da2a207afa1f549cb74524b3b4fd998c233bc1c366f72d87a4bca1fa7e54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162322" :question/id #uuid "9a894708-53b5-506d-832d-c14b94f740a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162322" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{{e}^{z}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{p}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,z)}^{{u}_{4}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le {e}^{z},\\,\\,{z}^{2}-1\\le y\\le x+z}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{{e}^{z}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/41b06d98498273a03b9df8eeaa60f4e74cb00168e8dd0b3b395c5b0a23518e57.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le {e}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/07a37b90df8b37d07c40af03ecba8e665797cfff368758db7ae17e45f11683cf.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=0\\,$ and $\\,z={x}^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,$ and $\\,z=1.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-1}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-162322/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162322"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162323/selection/e1854f60022ba6e116872aed601f98fa747a5ae3f4dfd075bf1907eb43e7f345" :answer/id #uuid "b4e5d935-b1d8-588b-9d18-fcb24789f84c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=2,\\,\\,\\,q=-2,\\,\\,\\,r=-\\sqrt{2-z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162323/selection/65f3bd3bf8fc9167ec1a4ee9032a5fcc66ea08e9b68b7b1b443e17e939ec7296" :answer/id #uuid "67e4403b-b393-5098-84a8-88bda050b7f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=-z,\\,\\,\\,r=-\\sqrt{z-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162323/selection/96d9a56b82c94ce7d7d18dfff660d73a1f698790bb378085dd14a2df9cf17340" :answer/id #uuid "bc065ed8-cec9-599b-a195-810525817ba7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=2,\\,\\,\\,q=-1,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162323/selection/8e6735e6ef09e7d606588647aaa22aa78d15b83937c023738d00bf055eaa9360" :answer/id #uuid "7fbfb73c-277b-5f32-b29a-ecab920060b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=-z,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162323/selection/63d0eefc1dbd67ca290fdffa50331f29f6a5da70947349de9d890642635e1b4d" :answer/id #uuid "efedd5b1-5f3d-598f-9286-d59f1564312c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=-1,\\,\\,\\,r=-\\sqrt{2-z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162323/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa84908-e6d1-586f-b7f1-655f86bff1eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162323/selection/63d0eefc1dbd67ca290fdffa50331f29f6a5da70947349de9d890642635e1b4d" :answer-field/choices ["q-162323/selection/e1854f60022ba6e116872aed601f98fa747a5ae3f4dfd075bf1907eb43e7f345" "q-162323/selection/65f3bd3bf8fc9167ec1a4ee9032a5fcc66ea08e9b68b7b1b443e17e939ec7296" "q-162323/selection/96d9a56b82c94ce7d7d18dfff660d73a1f698790bb378085dd14a2df9cf17340" "q-162323/selection/8e6735e6ef09e7d606588647aaa22aa78d15b83937c023738d00bf055eaa9360" "q-162323/selection/63d0eefc1dbd67ca290fdffa50331f29f6a5da70947349de9d890642635e1b4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162323" :question/id #uuid "3c6a7892-f379-54dc-a908-63706fadb5c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162323" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{(x+1)^{3}}^{2-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{p}{\\int }_{q}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}-1}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{r}^{0}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,z)}^{{u}_{4}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,(x+1)^{3}\\le z\\le 2-{x}^{2},\\,\\,x+z\\le y\\le z+2}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{(x+1)^{3}}^{2-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/5f586edef4202e3f3de09fe947c955de62efa35e421e85b3e5034efd375790a7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,(x+1)^{3}\\le z\\le 2-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/ec9845e347959ff1ac43a12d03261b1eb31172f14b56269d7bbfcb86a2a23e48.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{z}-1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-\\sqrt{2-z}\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,-1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt{2-z}\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}-1}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-z}}^{0}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-162323/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162323"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162325/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a661aee9-f867-561e-b915-e02e3325e715" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162325/selection/f0b17a5464291afc2aa40855590d2ce421a0a3765123093bbd5487b518e68d01" :answer/id #uuid "32f8f828-b742-5201-8bc1-9b31d3754291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162325/selection/64abd542708e9408a7f28dd5703f838e05fbf0069e113836b14b52b11c3fcec0" :answer/id #uuid "dadf3d2b-7ec9-560d-a4d8-80219b61e0bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162325/selection/2a28a53216361474017108aa9cf0fffd0fb392524d53aeb00b78355e4fa703c6" :answer/id #uuid "5690a51c-c8ed-5722-b7f4-04339169433a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162325/selection/cf60bdf0310731009b56663194c647265b54eebc087185b30be4afcacd9318be" :answer/id #uuid "07861a7f-023d-5351-92ca-12edc36ffd9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(e-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162325/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0170ff8-6b02-576b-8f7d-de381dbc8e3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162325/selection/f0b17a5464291afc2aa40855590d2ce421a0a3765123093bbd5487b518e68d01" :answer-field/choices ["q-162325/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-162325/selection/f0b17a5464291afc2aa40855590d2ce421a0a3765123093bbd5487b518e68d01" "q-162325/selection/64abd542708e9408a7f28dd5703f838e05fbf0069e113836b14b52b11c3fcec0" "q-162325/selection/2a28a53216361474017108aa9cf0fffd0fb392524d53aeb00b78355e4fa703c6" "q-162325/selection/cf60bdf0310731009b56663194c647265b54eebc087185b30be4afcacd9318be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162325" :question/id #uuid "5c2b5614-0c1e-5ce3-8b80-1b84145bbf51" :question/math-academy-id "q-162325" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,z\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/54d8420870ea92e42811a13ed2c4563c5c9ccb674ee8c3b6a277186c0fba21da.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,z\\le x\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=z\\,⟹\\,z=x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081417/assets/7f19b63139a3d38c321e9938c1e8fbbf84e924df51d9b9b057d0151fc269550d.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[z{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}z[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}z({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1){\\int }_{0}^{x}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1){[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[e-1-1+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162325/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162325"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
